\f1\fs24 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 <$Scr_H::2>
\f0\fs22 SARA Device and Procedure Overview
\f2\b\fs36 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 <!$Scr_H::2>
\f0\fs22 SARA
\f3\fs24 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec4  (SQUID Array for Reproductive Assessment) is a specialized fetal magnetoencephalography (fMEG) device developed at the University of Arkansas for Medical Sciences (UAMS) for noninvasive assessment of fetal brain activity{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medicine.uams.edu/obgyn/research/sara-fetal-meg-research-centers/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 1}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881832/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 2}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medicine.uams.edu/obgyn/research/sara-research-centers/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 4}}. It is the first instrument designed specifically to record multiple physiological signals from a woman\'92s uterus, including fetal brain, heart, and uterine activity{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medicine.uams.edu/obgyn/research/sara-fetal-meg-research-centers/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 1}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881832/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 2}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medicine.uams.edu/obgyn/research/sara-research-centers/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 4}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 <$Scr_H::2>
\f0\fs22 Device Description
\f2\b\fs36 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec4 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\'95	}<!$Scr_H::2>
\f0\fs22 	\'95	\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Sensor Array
\f2\b\fs24 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec4 :
\f3\b0  SARA features an array of 151 superconducting quantum interference device (SQUID) sensors, curved to fit the maternal abdomen from the perineum to the uterine fundus{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881832/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 2}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medicine.uams.edu/obgyn/research/sara-research-centers/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 4}}. These sensors detect extremely weak magnetic fields generated by electrical activity in fetal tissues, particularly the brain{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medicine.uams.edu/obgyn/research/sara-fetal-meg-research-centers/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 1}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medicine.uams.edu/obgyn/research/sara-research-centers/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 4}}.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\fs22 \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Reference Sensors
\f2\b\fs24 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec4 :
\f3\b0  An additional 29 reference SQUID sensors are positioned away from the main array to measure and help filter out ambient environmental magnetic noise{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881832/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 2}}.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\fs22 \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Shielding
\f2\b\fs24 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec4 :
\f3\b0  The mother sits against a concave shield that covers her abdomen, optimizing sensor contact and minimizing interference{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medicine.uams.edu/obgyn/research/sara-fetal-meg-research-centers/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 1}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 <$Scr_H::2>
\f0\fs22 Procedure
\f2\b\fs36 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec4 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}<!$Scr_H::2>
\f0\fs22 	1	\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Maternal Positioning
\f2\b\fs24 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec4 :
\f3\b0  The pregnant woman sits comfortably, leaning forward so her abdomen rests on the sensor array{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2830651/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 3}}.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\fs22 \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Localization
\f2\b\fs24 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec4 :
\f3\b0  Three localization coils are attached to specific points on the mother\'92s abdomen (right side, left side, and spine at the umbilicus) to determine the position and orientation relative to the sensor array. A portable ultrasound is used to locate the fetal head, and a fourth coil is placed over this spot for precise mapping{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881832/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 2}}.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\fs22 \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Recording Session
\f2\b\fs24 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec4 :
\f3\b0 \
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl1\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	
\f4 \uc0\u9702 
\f3 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Sessions typically last 6\'9612 minutes, with continuous data collection at a sampling rate of 312.5 Hz and a frequency band from DC to 100 Hz{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881832/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 2}}.\
\ls2\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	
\f4 \uc0\u9702 
\f3 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 The device can record spontaneous fetal brain activity as well as responses to auditory (sounds) and visual (light flashes) stimuli transmitted through the maternal abdomen{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medicine.uams.edu/obgyn/research/sara-fetal-meg-research-centers/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 1}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2830651/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 3}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medicine.uams.edu/obgyn/research/sara-research-centers/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 4}}.\
\ls2\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	
\f4 \uc0\u9702 
\f3 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 The system can simultaneously monitor fetal heart activity, uterine contractions, and fetal movements{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881832/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 2}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medicine.uams.edu/obgyn/research/sara-research-centers/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 4}}.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\fs22 \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Data Analysis
\f2\b\fs24 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec4 :
\f3\b0  Advanced signal processing techniques (such as filtering, averaging, and spatial filtering) are used to isolate fetal brain signals from maternal and environmental noise{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medicine.uams.edu/obgyn/research/sara-research-centers/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 4}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15498539/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 5}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 <$Scr_H::2>
\f0\fs22 Applications and Advantages
\f2\b\fs36 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec4 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\'95	}<!$Scr_H::2>
\f0\fs22 	\'95	\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Noninvasive
\f2\b\fs24 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec4 :
\f3\b0  No needles or invasive instruments are used, making it safe for both mother and fetus{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medicine.uams.edu/obgyn/research/sara-fetal-meg-research-centers/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 1}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881832/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 2}}.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\fs22 \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Direct Neurological Assessment
\f2\b\fs24 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec4 :
\f3\b0  SARA allows for direct, real-time monitoring of fetal neurological status, including detection of spontaneous and evoked brain activity{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881832/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 2}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2830651/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 3}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15498539/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 5}}.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\fs22 \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Clinical Utility
\f2\b\fs24 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec4 :
\f3\b0  It is particularly valuable for monitoring fetuses at risk of neurological injury due to maternal conditions like hypertension, diabetes, or growth restriction{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medicine.uams.edu/obgyn/research/sara-fetal-meg-research-centers/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 1}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881832/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 2}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medicine.uams.edu/obgyn/research/sara-research-centers/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 4}}.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\fs22 \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Research
\f2\b\fs24 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec4 :
\f3\b0  SARA has enabled studies of fetal sensory processing (auditory and visual), spontaneous brain rhythms, and even cardiac assessment, providing unique insights into fetal development and potential early markers of neurological disorders{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881832/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 2}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2830651/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 3}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medicine.uams.edu/obgyn/research/sara-research-centers/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 4}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15498539/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 5}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 <$Scr_H::2>
\f0\fs22 Summary
\f2\b\fs36 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 <!$Scr_H::2>
\f3 SARA represents a significant advance in fetal medicine, offering a noninvasive, high-resolution method for assessing fetal brain function and other physiological signals in utero. Its ability to detect subtle magnetic fields enables clinicians and researchers to monitor fetal neurological health and development, especially in high-risk pregnancies{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medicine.uams.edu/obgyn/research/sara-fetal-meg-research-centers/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 1}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881832/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 2}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2830651/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 3}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medicine.uams.edu/obgyn/research/sara-research-centers/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 4}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15498539/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 5}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

